[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Router4 is up and functioning normally. On the router itself, interface eth0 is UP with 192.168.200.1/24 assigned (the Location C gateway), and the WAN/uplink interfaces eth4 (192.168.15.2/24) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24) are also UP. The routing table is populated (several routes learned via RIP) and a default route via 172.25.1.2 on eth5 is present.

From the user VLAN, all four Location C hosts (PC11–PC14) can ping 192.168.200.1 successfully with sub-millisecond RTTs, confirming the gateway responds to ICMP on the local segment. Additionally, reachability tests show PCs in Location C can reach a host at another site (pc5, 172.16.10.5), proving that forwarding off-subnet through router4 is working as expected.

Localization: Router4 eth0 (to switch4), eth4 (to router5), and eth5 (to router3) are all operational. No interfaces are administratively down on the data path, and no errors are observed.

Mechanism: Because router4’s L3 interfaces are up and routes are present, both local gateway service and inter-site routing are functioning; if a user briefly lost connectivity, it would not have been caused by a router4 outage based on current evidence.

Root cause: No fault found on router4; the “router4 is down” report is not reproducible.

Corrective fix: No action required on router4. If issues persist for specific users, investigate client-side configuration or local switch ports (e.g., ARP cache, IP settings, cabling) rather than the router. 
=== END ===